WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.520
- I'm very much looking
forward to this video

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.700
because today's topic is both quite juicy

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.120
but also highly educational.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.530
And inspiration for today's topic,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.740
was coming from a Harvard
business review article

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.940
that was reading, the article is called,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.130
How Consultants Project Expertise

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.560
and Learn at the Same Time.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.680
I will link the article
in the video description.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.420
I do think it is quite insightful

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.270
and it pretty much asked the question,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.460
are all consultants conman?

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.560
Are they sincere?

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.570
What tactics do they apply to potentially

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.640
create an impression from the
client that is not accurate?

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.450
And as an introduction to the topic,

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.587
I want to read out to you
one paragraph of the article.

00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:44.840
"The challenge with effective
consulting is that it depends

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.070
on in-depth situational
knowledge that consultants

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.340
simply can't have when
they start an assignment.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.220
What's more, they may not
yet be completely clear

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.100
on what the client -
who's paying top dollars

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.200
and expect results
immediately - really wants.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.150
So consultants must rapidly and discreetly

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.130
gain knowledge of the client's business

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.295
while simultaneously giving an impression

00:01:07.295 --> 00:01:10.470
of competence and self-confidence.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.870
We call this challenge,
learning credibility tension."

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.200
And this is indeed a
challenge that you almost

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.250
always face as a consultant
if you start on a new project.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.630
Because on the one hand,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.110
the client expects you
to be super competent,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.440
highly knowledgeable on
the topic from day one,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.170
from the very first day you set forth

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:31.410
at the client's office.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.280
But on the other hand, you might be young,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.620
you might come fresh out of University.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.300
Maybe you did a similar
project in the past,

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.740
for sure you do not know in detail,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.640
the client's current processes.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.020
So how can it be perceived

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.560
as credible and confident by the client,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.180
if actually you are not.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.000
If actually you might
need a couple of weeks,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.610
a couple of months to get up to speed,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.300
to really get all the knowledge

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.320
and learnings that you need to really come

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.070
to a great and valuable
recommendation for the client.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.470
So this is the topic we're
going to talk about today.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.300
So welcome to another coffee break

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.610
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.730
My name is Heinrich, I'm a
former strategy consultant

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.040
and I want to help you
to become successful

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.580
in the first years of your career.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.980
Now, before we jump into this,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.260
I know that this topic is
a little bit controversial.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.190
And after watching this video, some of you

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.250
might really indeed think that
consulting is a conman job.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.083
This is not really what I want
to say here with this video,

00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:28.670
this is not my position.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.840
And I also want to open
with a little disclaimer

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.080
that all the things that
I would talk about now

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.919
are to my knowledge,
not officially endorsed

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:37.940
by any large consulting firm,

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.120
for sure not by the employers
that I worked for in the past.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.270
And these things are also not part

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.850
of any official training, curriculums,

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.640
of any of these large consulting firms

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.473
that I'm aware of.

00:02:48.473 --> 00:02:51.420
This is a rather practical,
street-smartness,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.470
type of knowledge type of tips

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.210
that you will pick up by
doing this job over time.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.010
So let's start by putting ourselves

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.630
into the shoes of a consultant.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.600
You're sitting at the client
in your first one or two weeks

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.880
of the project, and
you need to learn more.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.390
You need to learn more
about the client's business,

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.960
the client's industry,

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.800
what exactly the problem
is that you need to solve,

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.630
how the client's
processes work, and so on.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.010
But what can you do?

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.120
Of course you can just
blatantly ask the client.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.340
What does this mean?

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.390
How does this work?

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.430
How is this supposed to go?

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.310
But if you do this,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.570
if you ask these type of direct questions,

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.570
you will often get not so
nice answers from the clients.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.400
They might say, "Well, this
is why I hired consultants,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.070
for them to give me the answers.

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:35.350
I shouldn't train you.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.090
You should know these things.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.120
Why are we paying you so much money

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.540
if you're not even aware
of these basic things

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.610
of our business, of our
industry?" And so on.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.970
And trust me that these are not

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.468
some theoretical considerations,

00:03:46.468 --> 00:03:50.880
you'll hear things like that
all the time on projects,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.360
these things happen quite frequently.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.890
And indeed a partner I
worked with in the past

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.570
once taught me, "Well, Heinrich,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.790
consulting is like close quarter combat.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:04.250
You really need to be careful,
need to watch every step

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.600
behind every corner, behind every door,

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.940
there might be danger, be careful."

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.889
So it might be fair to
argue that as a consultant,

00:04:12.889 --> 00:04:15.720
often you need to fake it till you make it

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.950
in the first week, so in the first months,

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.820
you somehow need to
pretend to be knowledgeable

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.280
to be an expert, until you really build up

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.710
that knowledge over time.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.980
And this is now where the
juicy part of this video begins

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:31.360
because now I want to share
four of these sneaky tactics

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.586
that you could employ as a consultant

00:04:33.586 --> 00:04:35.770
to create exactly this impression.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.210
And again, I do not say
that every consultant

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.720
is using techniques like this,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.030
and also do not necessarily
endorse to behave in this way.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.480
But let just say the tactics like this

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.240
can be quite helpful
in certain situations.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.910
And the first tactic
is to leverage the fact

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.001
that you as a consultant
have an opportunity

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:56.100
that many other employees
of the company do not have.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.370
And this is that you,
as part of the project,

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.770
will very often be able to talk
to lots of different people

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.640
at the client, from lots
of different departments.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.970
And by that you will
collect lots of information

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.990
that probably many employees
will not be able to have,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.970
if they just work on one specific task

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.560
and one specific department
for their whole career.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.700
So often by talking to one client,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.150
you will pick up some industry trends,

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.100
some certain specific information

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.227
on what is going on in the company,

00:05:24.227 --> 00:05:26.630
that probably another client of yours,

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.980
and another employee of the
company, is not really aware of.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.370
So the next time you then
talk to this other person

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.910
at the client, you can
then selectively share some

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:37.400
of the information that you
got from the first client.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.990
And by that, because this other client

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:40.310
might not be aware of that,

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.850
he or she will then often
perceive you as very competent,

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.350
very well-informed, that
you really know your things,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.930
if you tell him information
about their company

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.400
that even they do not know.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.627
And indeed, there's this
sarcastic saying that

00:05:52.627 --> 00:05:55.960
"Consultant just borrow your
watch to tell you the time."

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.120
And while of course,
that over simplifies it,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.550
I do think that there
is some truth to that

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.400
in the sense that this works
up to the highest levels.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.200
Often after a project in the end

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.710
that if you already
present your final results

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.670
to the board, to the C level,

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.660
in your recommendation
it will not be unusual.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.120
That is some of the
recommendations that you make

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.290
will be ideas that you more
or less directly derived

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:21.230
from information that you
got from other people working

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.300
in the organization, from
people really working

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.150
in the machine room, that just
shared some ideas with you

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.360
on what the company
needs to do to improve.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.630
And to not digress too far
from the topic of this video,

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:33.600
I do not want to debate
the merits of this.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.360
I do think though, that there
is an argument to be made

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.140
that even if consultants do that,

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.670
there still can be a significant value add

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.500
in the way that consultants do this.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.910
But again, let's maybe part
this for another video.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.970
The second sneaky tactic that
I want to talk about today

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.370
is just how you frame your information,

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.310
and specifically to frame
information that you have

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.550
as your very own experiences.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.240
So let's imagine that you
indeed do some research

00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.930
on the client, on the industry,

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.670
before you start the project
and you may be read a book

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.560
or report about specific things,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.480
maybe you even talk to some experts,

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.710
some other people, some senior partners.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:11.160
So you do know some things about the topic

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.280
that now you need to work on.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.800
But of course the problem here is

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.000
if you're having a debate with a client,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.260
and you want to share some
pieces of information,

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.680
and you just say, "Oh yeah,
I read this in a book"

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.400
or "I read this in a report."

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.450
Of course this will often
not be very convincing.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:26.933
This is will not really portray you

00:07:26.933 --> 00:07:29.270
as a big expert on a certain topic.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:32.253
So instead you can frame
this as your own experiences,

00:07:32.253 --> 00:07:35.760
you can say, "Oh yes, last
time I worked on this,

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:36.920
this is how we did it."

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.800
You can say, "From my experience,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.297
this is how this is handled."

00:07:40.297 --> 00:07:42.417
"This is how you usually this works."

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:45.150
"This is what other
companies in this field do."

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.810
And this frames you as
not only being book-smart,

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.520
it's not only being a student

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.560
who read about a certain
topic in the past,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.350
but as being a professional,

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.370
as being an expert who has done
similar things in the past,

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.860
who has worked on these specific topics,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.850
on prior engagement, on
prior jobs, or whatever.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.460
My third little sneaky tactic
is on how to frame questions.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:06.770
And actually this I picked up

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.609
in one of my very first internships.

00:08:08.609 --> 00:08:10.690
I was working with a project lead

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.710
and she always seemed super confident.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:15.780
She always seemed like she
really would know her things.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.827
So one day I just asked her as an intern,

00:08:17.827 --> 00:08:18.810
"How do you do this?

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.300
You always seem so confident.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.680
No, clearly these topics need to be new

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.910
to you at least to some extent as way."

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:26.500
So I asked her, "How do you do this?"

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.270
And she indeed shared one little tactic

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:30.160
with me that I really found helpful

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.310
and frankly also applied
several times myself

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.143
then afterwards.

00:08:33.143 --> 00:08:33.976
And she told me,

00:08:33.976 --> 00:08:37.040
whenever a client uses a term
that you do not understand

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:39.670
or say something that is
not really clear to you,

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:41.980
instead of asking, what does this mean?

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:43.440
What does this term mean?

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:44.550
Instead of asking this,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.060
because these questions of course

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.920
potentially make you look ignorant.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.520
Instead, rather ask what
do you mean with this term?

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.000
What does this mean to you?

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.460
What exactly do you mean if you say this?

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.970
Because what this implies

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:00.020
is that not only you
understand the general meaning

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.820
of what this person was talking about,

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.193
but even have this higher
more abstract knowledge

00:09:04.193 --> 00:09:08.100
that you are aware that not
everybody in every company

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.640
is using this term in the very same way.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.824
So now you want to know
specifically how this client

00:09:13.824 --> 00:09:16.310
and this company is using this.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:18.200
So you completely turn around the frame

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.180
from you have no idea at all

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.400
about what this person was talking about,

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.670
to you really completely
understanding this,

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.440
but now you really want to understand

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.400
how exactly this is meant,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.550
this is to be understood in this company,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.430
for this client, for this person.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.850
So the fourth tactic is
maybe the most sneaky one.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.410
And I even asked myself
whether I should include it

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.410
but I want to be open
and transparent with you

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.560
because I do know that
some people do this,

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.250
but reflect on this for yourself

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:44.850
and only use at your own discretion.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:48.050
And this tactic can help you
to be perceived as intelligent.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.642
Of course, the client, when
he or she hires consultants,

00:09:50.642 --> 00:09:53.499
especially when the client
hires MBB consultants,

00:09:53.499 --> 00:09:55.617
everybody knows what the fees are

00:09:55.617 --> 00:09:57.850
for these type of professionals.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:01.357
They expect to work with
highly intelligent people.

00:10:01.357 --> 00:10:02.710
And of course it might help you

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.420
if you are indeed perceived
to be intelligent.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.400
But how can you signal
that you are intelligent?

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.490
And indeed, at least from my experience,

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.460
one of the strongest signals

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:16.260
that always have a very
strong effect is mental math.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.150
If you're really good at mental math,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.840
everybody would think that
you are highly intelligent.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:23.140
Interestingly, this is
not only true for clients

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:24.860
but for your own team, for your partner,

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.680
your project lead, you work with this way.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.183
And of course, ideally you
really are good with mental math.

00:10:29.183 --> 00:10:30.900
If this is really easy to you,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.900
and then you can indeed impress people

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.960
just by doing some crazy
calculations in your head.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.230
So what do you do if actually

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.970
your mental math is really bad?

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:40.780
You're just not good at all of it.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:43.530
So what you could do is when
you prepare for a meeting,

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.330
and when you know that you will present

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.270
for instance some parts of a document,

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:48.640
or maybe a whole document,

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.539
some pages of a PowerPoint presentation,

00:10:50.539 --> 00:10:53.370
then already think about
are there some pages

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:56.080
with numbers on them and then potentially

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.670
you could do some little
calculations on your presentation,

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.000
that would seem quite impressive.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.400
Because then what you can do is

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.500
that you can fake the mental math.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.420
So before you walk into the meeting,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.010
you think of a calculation,

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.800
you already calculate the result

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.850
with Excel or with the calculator.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:13.510
And then you walk into the meeting,

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:17.140
you present the document
and then live, on the spot,

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:19.030
you pretend that you're
calculating something

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.410
saying, okay so you multiply this by this,

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:22.630
and then divide it by this,

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.330
then this is the number
that you would get.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:27.400
So again, yes, this is
probably more than just sneaky.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.640
So take responsibility
for your own actions

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.750
and decide whether you really
want to do this or not.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:33.620
So now reflecting on these tactics

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:34.890
and I'll also want to make a clear

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:38.070
that these tactics are not
only relevant for consultants

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.934
but also for temporary
workers, for freelancers,

00:11:40.934 --> 00:11:43.300
for other professional advisors.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.750
These are just things that
are to some extent necessary

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:50.600
to bridge the so-called
learning credibility tension

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.230
that the article is describing.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:53.456
To brush this tension,

00:11:53.456 --> 00:11:54.690
from the very beginning,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:56.830
you just need to be perceived as credible

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:58.510
if you want to succeed in your job.

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:00.760
But then at the same time
you also need the time

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.630
to get some learning from the client

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:05.090
to really get up to speed
and really understand

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.360
